# Theme shortcuts: subtheme

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`theme_sub_axis()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/subtheme.html), which covers
`theme_sub_axis_x()`, `theme_sub_axis_y()`, `theme_sub_axis_bottom()`, `theme_sub_axis_top()`,
`theme_sub_axis_left()`, `theme_sub_axis_right()`, `theme_sub_legend()`, `theme_sub_panel()`,
`theme_sub_plot()` and `theme_sub_strip()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example
shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2
code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

These functions are shortcuts for `theme()` with shorter argument names: each sets the elements of
one part of the plot, so `theme_sub_axis_bottom(title = ...)` sets what `theme(axis.title.x.bottom =
...)` sets. There is one for all the axes, for both x axes, for both y axes and for each of the
four, and one each for the legend, the panels, the whole plot and the facet strips. Giggly sets the
same elements with `Giggly.Plot.theme/2`, but has none of these functions
(`theme_sub_axis_bottom()`). A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells
write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The data is `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor
Trend* US magazine, which the setup cell binds to `mtcars`, R's name for it.

## Styling the bottom axis with `theme()`

```r
# A standard plot
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(disp, mpg, colour = drat)) +
  geom_point()

red_text <- element_text(colour = "red")
red_line <- element_line(colour = "red")

# The theme settings below:
p + theme(
  axis.title.x.bottom = red_text,
  axis.text.x.bottom  = red_text,
  axis.line.x.bottom  = red_line,
  axis.ticks.x.bottom = red_line
)
```

An element is a keyword list of its properties, so `red_text` and `red_line` are keyword lists.

```elixir
p =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :disp, y: :mpg, colour: :drat})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

red_text = [colour: "red"]
red_line = [colour: "red"]

p
|> Plot.theme(
  axis_title_x_bottom: red_text,
  axis_text_x_bottom: red_text,
  axis_line_x_bottom: red_line,
  axis_ticks_x_bottom: red_line
)
|> render.()
```

## The same with `theme_sub_axis_bottom()`

```r
# Are equivalent to these less verbose theme settings
p + theme_sub_axis_bottom(
  title = red_text,
  text  = red_text,
  line  = red_line,
  ticks = red_line
)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `theme_sub_axis_bottom()`.